00:00:06.928 --> 00:00:09.220
DALE MARKOWITZ: I think the
best types of relationships

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.500
between mentors and
mentees are ones

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.410
where there's a really
unique fit between the two.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:15.890
What do I mean by that?

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.670
Well, I get a lot of requests,
people asking for mentorship,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.065
where they want to know how
do I succeed in data science?

00:00:21.065 --> 00:00:22.690
Or, how to learn
machine learning more?

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.773
Please mentor me, give me
product ideas, whatever.

00:00:24.773 --> 00:00:25.970
Something really broad.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:30.160
And I just think, there are
potentially many good mentors

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.870
for this person,
but I'm not sure

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.668
that I'm particularly
well-suited to help.

00:00:33.668 --> 00:00:35.710
STEPHANIE WONG: When you're
looking for a mentor,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.540
make sure you do
your due diligence

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.430
by coming to the
table with research,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.040
either about that person
or about the technology

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.700
that you'd like some help on.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.400
So check out the
person on LinkedIn,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.320
find out what their
experience is.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.290
Or maybe you want to first
look at a white paper

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.180
or do some research
in that area,

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.890
and come to the
conversation with points

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.270
that you'd like
their opinion on.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.853
TULSEE DOSHI: So my
advice for finding

00:00:56.853 --> 00:00:59.440
a mentor in the tech industry
is honestly just to ask.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.100
I think there are so many
amazing smart people who

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.260
would be more than
happy to give advice

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.653
and to share their learnings
and their knowledge.

00:01:07.653 --> 00:01:09.070
RACHEL BEEN: Find
the person who's

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.290
willing to bring you
into a really, really

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.390
tough conversation.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.710
A really, really senior
person without any sort

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.145
of preparation, but they trust
you enough to handle your own,

00:01:18.145 --> 00:01:19.803
I think that's
the type of person

00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:21.220
that you might
want to learn from.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.762
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Be very respectful

00:01:22.762 --> 00:01:24.320
of your mentor's time.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.060
Once you have identified
the person who inspires you

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.290
the most, who you
would want to be,

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.810
then reach out over
email or social media

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.330
with specific guidance
on the exact thing

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.890
that you're looking
to accomplish.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:37.960
I find the more
details you provide,

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.767
it makes it easier for
you to receive help.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.850
ALEXANDRINA GARCIA-VERDIN:
Find people you admire.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.850
People you're
really inspired by.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.015
And do some research
on the things

00:01:48.015 --> 00:01:49.390
they have written
on the internet

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.780
so that they don't have
to re-explain basics,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.110
since they probably already
shared these things online.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.300
And after understanding
what it is

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.390
that you want to learn from
these folks, reach out to them

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:06.220
for virtual coffee if they're in
your organization's directory.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.410
If they are external,
reach out to them

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.539
via platforms like LinkedIn.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.090
And once you build a rapport,
keep chatting with them

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.260
once a quarter.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.110
SHILPA VIR: The way to find
good mentor in the tech industry

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.850
is to just be open.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.700
And approaching people
that you admire,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.100
even if it's just
one small thing,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.760
and asking them if you
can spend some time

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.352
with them learning about it.

00:02:29.352 --> 00:02:31.810
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Rather than
having one mentor that I go to

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.790
for questions
across the board, I

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.430
tend to seek out those I
admire in specific areas.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:40.330
So for example, when I was
looking at moving careers,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.250
I talked to a co-worker
that had actually

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.950
made a really similar move,
and got advice from her

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.980
on how to get started.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.880
And in another situation
when I was really

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.870
hesitating for this big,
risky, but good decision,

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.200
I talked to another co-worker
who I find absolutely fearless,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.330
and she definitely
helped talk me into it.

